ash for Debian and Ubuntu
=========================

Why this package needs a Python index at install time
-----------------------------------------------------

The package bundles ASH's own wheel and nothing else. Its post-install step creates
a virtualenv under /usr/lib/ash/venv and installs that wheel into it, which resolves
ASH's 27 runtime dependencies from whatever Python index the host is configured for.

This is not an oversight, and bundling the dependency wheels would not be an
improvement. One of those dependencies, detect-secrets, is itself a security scanner.
The project forbids any published artifact from containing third-party scanner source,
and a .deb carrying a detect_secrets wheel would contain exactly that. ASH's published
wheel satisfies the rule only because it declares its dependencies rather than
vendoring them, and this package inherits the guarantee only by doing the same.

On a host with no reachable index, stage one before installing:

  pip download automated-security-helper==<version> -d /srv/ash-wheels
  # then, on the target, before apt-get install:
  printf 'no-index = true\nfind-links = /srv/ash-wheels\n' >> /etc/pip.conf

Scanners are not installed by this package
------------------------------------------

ASH drives third-party scanners; it does not contain them. After installing:

  ash dependencies install              # everything ASH can provision
  ash dependencies install --tool grype # or one at a time

Files
-----

  /usr/lib/ash/wheels/   ASH's wheel, the package's only payload
  /usr/lib/ash/venv/     created by postinst, removed by prerm
  /usr/bin/ash           wrapper that execs the venv entry point

The venv is built on the target rather than shipped, because a virtualenv records
absolute paths and the interpreter ABI of the machine that created it.
